## Reagent setup
## Deionized water (DIW)
DIW is produced by the Milli-Q water purification system in all experiments. The quality of DIW is critical for the synthesis of ZIFs and electrochemical performance tests.
## ZIF-8 stock dispersion $\mathbf{\left(0.100\g/mL\right)}$
ZIF-8 stock dispersion $\left(0.100\ \mathrm{g/mL}\right)$ is used in the synthesis of HZIF-8 and ZIF- $8@\mathrm{{mPDA}}$ . ZIF-8 particles obtained by using the water-based synthesis method are used to prepare a ZIF-8 dispersion.
1 Wash an $11\mathrm{-mL}$ glass vial with detergent and tap water.   
2 Soak the vial in $10\%$ (wt/wt) $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution for $10~\mathrm{min}$ and rinse it thoroughly with DIW.   
3 Place the cleaned $11\mathrm{-mL}$ glass vial in an air-forced oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ until fully dried.   
4 After drying is complete, add $\mathbf{1.00\g}$ of finely ground ZIF-8 ${\sim}120$ and ${\sim}200~\mathrm{nm}$ for HZIF-8 and ZIF- $\cdot8@\mathrm{mPDA}$ syntheses, respectively) and then $10~\mathrm{\mL}$ of DIW into the $11\mathrm{-mL}$ glass vial. CRITICAL When adding DIW, try to rinse down any particles adhering to the wall of the vial.   
5 Sonicate the mixture for $30\mathrm{min}$ to obtain a homogeneous ZIF-8 dispersion. CRITICAL To ensure homogeneous dispersion of ZIF-8 particles in DIW, gently shake the dispersion of ZIF-8 in the 11- mL glass vial with sonication for 3 min and then leave it under sonication for an additional $27\mathrm{min}$ .   
6 Close the vial tightly with its cap and seal the gap between the vial and the cap with Parafilm. CRITICAL It is highly recommended to prepare fresh ZIF-8 dispersion before each experiment to ensure the quality of ZIF-8 particles. However, a maximum storage period of 1 week is allowed if stored at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ CRITICAL If ZIF-8 particles sediment at the bottom of the vial after standing for some time, sonicate the dispersion for $5~\mathrm{min}$ to redisperse ZIF-8 before use.
## ${\bf H}_{2}{\bf S O}_{4}$ stock solution (1 M)
${\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{SO}}_{4}$ stock solution (1 M) is used as an electrolyte solution for supercapacitor measurements. The solution is prepared in a $50\mathrm{-mL}$ glass vial. The vial is first washed with detergent and tap water and then soaked in $10\%$ (wt/wt) $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution followed by thorough rinsing with DIW. The vial is dried in an air-forced oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After drying, $42.0~\mathrm{mL}$ of DIW is added to the vial by using 5,000- and $200\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{L}$ micropipettes. Subsequently, $2.42~\mathrm{mL}$ of $98\%$ (wt/wt) $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution is added in a dropwise manner to the DIW by using 1,000- and $200\mathrm{-}\upmu\mathrm{L}$ micropipettes. Close the vial loosely with its cap and gently swirl the vial. Open the vial to release any pressure that builds up. Repeat these processes until a homogeneous solution is obtained. Close the vial tightly with its cap and wrap Parafilm around the gap between the vial and cap. Store it in a refrigerator at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ ! CAUTION DIW should be added to the vial before the addition of $98\%$ (wt/wt) $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution to avoid excessive release of heat. ! CAUTION Mixing DIW and $98\%$ (wt/wt) $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution generates heat. The solution should not be stirred vigorously, and the vial cap should be closed initially only loosely. The solution should be carefully stirred with monitoring of the solution temperature. CRITICAL Wrapping the cap-vial gap with Parafilm and storing the stock solution at $4~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are important, because this prevents gradual evaporation of DIW and any possible changes in the concentration of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution. CRITICAL It is always recommended to use a freshly prepared electrolyte solution for each electrochemical measurement. CRITICAL Variation in concentration of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution causes variation in potential during the supercapacitor measurements.
## Electrode dispersion
The electrode dispersion consists of active materials (nanoporous carbons in this protocol), PVDF and acetylene black mixed at a fixed weight ratio in NMP. This is used to prepare the working electrode by a drop-casting method (Equipment setup; Fig. 1).
1 Wash a 4-mL glass vial with detergent and tap water.   
2 Soak the vial in $10\%$ (wt/wt) $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution for $10~\mathrm{min}$ and rinse it thoroughly with DIW.   
3 Place the cleaned 4-mL glass vial in an air-forced oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ until fully dried.   
4 After drying is complete, carefully weigh and transfer $10~\mathrm{mg}$ of finely ground nanoporous carbon,   
$1.25~\mathrm{mg}$ of PVDF and $1.25~\mathrm{mg}$ of acetylene carbon (8:1:1 in weight ratio) to the 4-mL glass vial.
CRITICAL Nanoporous carbons and acetylene carbons are first finely ground to avoid the formation of aggregates in the electrode dispersion.
5 Add $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of NMP to the $_\mathrm{4-mL}$ glass vial. The mixture of active materials, acetylene carbons and PVDF in NMP is sonicated for $^{\textrm{1h}}$ to achieve a highly homogeneous dispersion. For more details, see the Troubleshooting table. ! CAUTION Adhesion of carbon particles to the wall of the vial during the sonication must be minimized. CRITICAL When adding NMP, try to rinse down any particles sticking to the wall of the vial. CRITICAL Ensure that PVDF is fully dissolved in the electrode dispersion after sonication. CRITICAL It is always recommended to prepare a fresh electrode dispersion at the time of electrode fabrication.
## Equipment setup
## Glass equipment
All glassware used in this protocol is first washed by using a detergent and rinsed with tap water. Subsequently, the glassware is soaked in $10\%$ (wt/wt) $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ to dissolve any remaining contaminants at its surfaces. After acid treatment, the glassware is thoroughly rinsed with DIW and dried in an air-forced oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ ! CAUTION $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution is corrosive and can cause serious damage to the body by inhalation or physical contact. Acid treatment must be conducted carefully in a well-ventilated fume hood with appropriate personal protective equipment, including safety goggles, nitrile gloves and a laboratory coat.
## Tubular furnace setup
A tubular furnace is used for the direct carbonization of materials under a flow of the required gas such as nitrogen, argon or ammonia. In this protocol, all direct carbonization processes are conducted under a nitrogen atmosphere with the tubular furnace connected to a nitrogen gas line. The nitrogen gas flow rate is set to $140~\mathrm{mL/min}$ and monitored by using a flow meter situated in the nitrogen line before entering the alumina tube. The outlet of the nitrogen gas line is immersed in a water-filled beaker to confirm the exit of nitrogen gas from the tube by observing the nitrogen gas bubbling.
The tubular furnace is operated according to a three-step program. The first step is a ramping stage to the required annealing temperature (ranging from 700 to $1,000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in this protocol) and involves a steady heating rate (2 or $5~{^\circ}\mathrm{C}/{\mathrm{min}}$ in this protocol). The second step is an annealing stage, at which the required temperature is maintained for a specific time period (2 or 3 or $^{5\mathrm{~h~}}$ in this protocol). The third step is the cooling stage, in which heating is not applied, and the temperature returns to the ambient condition. CRITICAL If no bubbling of nitrogen gas is observed from the nitrogen outlet line, there is either a gas leak or a blockage in the outlet tube. In this case, the apparatus and tube must be checked for leaks and replaced, followed by a confirmation that nitrogen bubbling occurs throughout the heating process.
## Preparation of the graphite working electrode
1 Carefully polish the top surface of the graphite electrode ${\mathrm{1~cm}}\times1{\mathrm{~cm}} $ with sandpaper to flatten it and remove any contaminants. Rinse the graphite with ethanol and DIW and then dry it in an airforced oven at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .   
2 Apply Teflon tape tightly on every surface of the graphite working electrode except the top surface. Place the as-prepared graphite working electrode on a glass plate. CRITICAL Teflon tape should be applied tightly to concentrate the electrode dispersion only on the top surface of the graphite working electrode. CRITICAL When placing the graphite working electrode on the glass plate, ensure that the top surface is parallel to the surface of the glass plate.   
3 To prepare electrode dispersion, add $10.0~\mathrm{mg}$ of active materials, $1.25~\mathrm{mg}$ of CB, $1.25~\mathrm{mg}$ of PVDF and subsequently $1~\mathrm{mL}$ of NMP in a 4-mL glass vial. The ratio of NPC/CB/PVDF in the electrode dispersion is set at 8:1:1.   
4 Tightly close the $_\mathrm{4-mL}$ glass vial with its cap and sonicate the mixture for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ without stirring. CRITICAL Adsorbed active materials on the wall of the glass vial can lead to variations in the concentration of active materials in the electrode dispersion and eventual decreases in specific capacitance.   
5 After sonication, carefully drop-cast the first $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of electrode dispersion onto the exposed top surface of the graphite working electrode and dry it in an air-forced oven at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .   
6 After drying, carefully drop-cast the second $50~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of electrode dispersion onto the coated top surface of the graphite working electrode and dry it in an air-forced oven at $50~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^3\mathrm{h}$ . CRITICAL The weight loading of active material is $1~\mathrm{mg}$ per electrode.   
7 Leave the prepared graphite working electrode at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under vacuum overnight. For more details, see the Troubleshooting table.
## Supercapacitor measurement setup
The measurement setup consists of an electrochemical workstation, a voltammetry vial (volume of 20 mL with $40\mathrm{-mm}$ diameter and $74\mathrm{-mm}$ height) with a cap (Teflon cap with three holes of $6\mathrm{-mm}$ diameter) and 1 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution. The three-electrode electrochemical system consists of a $\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}$ reference electrode $_{(6-\mathrm{mm}}$ in diameter and $78\mathrm{-}\mathrm{mm}$ in length), a $\mathrm{Pt}$ counter electrode ( $23\mathrm{-cm}$ in length, coil-shaped) and the graphite electrode with copper wire as the working electrode.
1 Place the $\mathrm{\Ag/AgCl}$ reference electrode, the $\mathrm{Pt}$ counter electrode and graphite electrode coated with electrode materials individually through the holes of the Teflon cap.   
2 Close the voltammetry vial containing electrolyte solution with the Teflon cap/electrode assembly.   
3 Connect the electrodes to the electrochemical workstation by pinching their protruding parts with the alligator clips available on the appropriate workstation connector wires.   
4 Fill the space between the holes on the cap and the electrodes by using rubber gaskets or rubber-like materials (such as Bostik Blu Tack). CRITICAL The insulating oxide layer of copper wire must be removed (by scratching) before the measurement to allow current flow between the electrochemical workstation and the working electrode.
## SEM setup
Samples in Procedure 1 and 3 are imaged by using Hitachi S-4800 and SU-8000 microscopes, respectively, operating at an accelerating voltage of $5\mathrm{kV}$ . Samples in Procedure 2 and 4 are imaged by using a JEOL JSM-7100F microscope operating at accelerating voltage of $10~\mathrm{kV}$ . Si wafers $3\mathrm{mm}\times$ $3\mathrm{mm}$ ) are fixed to carbon tape attached to a sample holder. A volume of $7\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of sample dispersion is dropped onto the Si wafer, and the solvents are allowed to evaporate at room temperature. Samples are then stored in an oven-vacuum chamber at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize6h}$ and cleaned by plasma for $10\ \mathrm{min}$ to remove the residual contamination completely. The samples are sputtered with $\mathrm{Pt}$ to improve conductivity.
## TEM setup
Samples in Procedure 1 and 3 are imaged by using JEOL JEM-2100F and JEM-2100 microscopes, respectively, operating at an accelerating voltage of $200\mathrm{kV}$ . Samples in Procedure 2 and 4 are imaged by using a Hitachi HT7700 microscope operating at an accelerating voltage of $120\mathrm{kV}$ . A TEM grid is placed on a wafer carrier tray. A volume of $7\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of the sample dispersion is dropped onto the TEM grid, and the solvents are allowed to evaporate at room temperature.
## Specific capacitance calculation for supercapacitor
Specific capacitance in a three-electrode system can be calculated from the CV curves. For supercapacitor measurements, the CV curves are typically obtained in a selected fixed potential window with various scan rates in 1 M $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution. The specific capacitance of the active material can be calculated from the CV curve by using the following equation:
$$
C_{s p}=\frac{1}{2\times\nu\times\Delta V\times\Delta m}\int I\mathrm{d}V
$$
where $C_{\mathrm{sp}}$ is the specific capacitance $\mathrm{(F/g)}$ , $\nu$ is the scan rate $\mathrm{(V/s)}$ , $\Delta V$ is the potential window, $\Delta m$ is the mass of the active material (g) and $I\mathop{}\mathrm{d}V$ is the integrated area of the CV curve.
Specific capacitance in a three-electrode system can also be calculated with GCD curves. For the supercapacitor measurement, the GCD curves are collected for a selected fixed potential window at various current densities in $1\mathrm{M}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ solution. The specific capacitance of the active material can be calculated from the GCD curve by using the following equation:
$$
C_{s p}=\frac{I\times\Delta t}{\Delta V\times\Delta m}
$$
where $C_{\mathrm{sp}}$ is the specific capacitance $\mathrm{(F/g)}$ , $I$ is the current (A), $\Delta t$ is the discharge time (s), $\Delta V$ is the potential window (V) and $\Delta m$ is the mass of the active material $(\mathbf{g})$ .
## Procedure 1: synthesis of ZIF-8 and its direct carbonization
## Synthesis of ZIF-8 with diameter $\mathsf{\Gamma}{\sim}1.5\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ Timing $27\ h$
1 Add $0.176\mathrm{~g~}$ of zinc acetate dihydrate, $0.300\ \mathrm{g}$ of polyvinylpyrrolidone and then $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol into a $25\mathrm{-mL}$ glass vial. Sonicate the mixture for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ until the reagents are completely dissolved for the preparation of the zinc precursor solution.
2 Add $0.263\mathrm{~g~}$ of 2-methylimidazole and then $20~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol into a $150\mathrm{-mL}$ beaker. Stir the mixture at $400~\mathrm{rpm}$ with a magnetic stirring bar $_{40-\mathrm{{mm}}}$ length and $8\mathrm{-mm}$ diameter) for $10~\mathrm{min}$ until 2-methylimidazole has completely dissolved for preparation of the organic linker solution.
CRITICAL STEP Close the glass vial with its cap before sonication.
CRITICAL STEP Cover the beaker with Parafilm during stirring to minimize evaporation of methanol.
Pour the zinc precursor solution into the organic linker solution at a steady rate.
CRITICAL STEP Remove any air bubbles formed at the center of the mixture by using a disposable pipette to minimize potential disruption of nucleation and growth of ZIF-8.
4 Stir the mixture of the zinc precursor solution and the organic linker solution at $400~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ to initiate nucleation of ZIF-8 (Fig. 2a).
CRITICAL STEP Abrupt addition of the zinc precursor, more vigorous stirring and higher reaction temperatures can lead to the formation of polycrystalline ZIF-8 (Fig. 2b).
5 Stop stirring after $10\ \mathrm{\min}$ and remove the magnetic stirring bar ( $_{40-\mathrm{{mm}}}$ length and $8\mathrm{-mm}$ diameter).
Cover the beaker with two layers of Parafilm and age the mixture for $24\mathrm{h}$ at room temperature to allow sufficient time for the growth of single crystals of ZIF-8 (Fig. 2c).
7 After $24\mathrm{h},$ carefully discard the more-transparent top layer of solution and transfer the dense white bottom layer into $50\mathrm{-mL}$ centrifuge tubes.
8 Centrifuge the collected ZIF-8 dispersion at 3,273 r.c.f. for $20\mathrm{min}$ . Discard the supernatant solution and resuspend the white solid pellet in $40~\mathrm{mL}$ of DIW.
CRITICAL STEP To resuspend the pellet, first add $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of DIW, then crush it by using a spatula as much as possible and then add the remaining $30~\mathrm{mL}$ of DIW. With the cap closed, repeatedly alternately sonicate $(\sim30~\mathrm{s})$ and then shake the solution vigorously to further break the pellet.
9 Repeat the washing process (centrifuging, discarding and resuspending) five times with DIW (the total number of washing cycles is five).
$\blacktriangle$ CRITICAL STEP A small volume of ethanol ( ${\cdot}5~\mathrm{mL}$ ) can be added along with DIW for each washing process to ensure the formation of a pellet at the bottom of the tube.
10 After five washing cycles, finely grind the ZIF-8 pellet in a mortar and pestle, keeping the solid moist with DIW.
CRITICAL STEP To finely grind the ZIF-8 pellet, the volume of DIW added is recommended to be minimum to maintain a slurry-like state of the ZIF-8 dispersion.
11 After grinding, transfer the ZIF-8 dispersion from the mortar to $50\mathrm{-mL}$ glass vials. Cover the vials with aluminum foil and then stab the foil with a scalpel.
12 Place the $50\mathrm{-mL}$ glass vials containing the dispersion of ZIF-8 in an air-forced oven at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ until fully dried to obtain a white powder of ZIF-8 (Extended Data Fig. 2e).
PAUSE POINT The $11\mathrm{-mL}$ glass vial containing the as-synthesized ZIF-8 particles can be stored in the vacuum desiccator for later use.
## Direct carbonization of ZIF-8 ● Timing $12\ h$
13 Set the tubular furnace to increase its temperature at a steady heating rate ${\mathrm{?5^{\circ}C/m i n}})$ to reach the required annealing temperature (700, 800, 900 and $1,000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ and hold for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
4 Place the alumina tube in the tubular furnace and tightly close one end with the gas outlet.
CRITICAL STEP Ensure that the alumina tube is clean. To remove adsorbed carbon sources in the alumina tube, heat the alumina tube with both ends open at $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^3\mathrm{h}$ . After the alumina tube reaches room temperature, flow ethanol and DIW through the tube to remove any residues that might be present. It is recommended that the alumina tube be cleaned after each use.
15 Finely grind ZIF-8 in the mortar and pestle and carefully transfer $0.100\mathrm{~g~}$ of ZIF-8 to an alumina combustion boat.
$\blacktriangle$ CRITICAL STEP Spread the powder evenly across the surface of the alumina combustion boat to allow homogeneous heat transfer to ZIF-8 particles during the direct carbonization.
16 Carefully place the alumina combustion boat containing $0.100\mathrm{~g~}$ of ZIF-8 into the open end of the alumina tube and slowly push it to the heating zone of the tubular furnace by using a stainlesssteel hook.
17 Tightly close the open end of the alumina tube with the gas inlet and open the nitrogen gas line until nitrogen gas flows through the flowmeter at $140\ \mathrm{mL/min}$ .
! CAUTION Ensure that there are no nitrogen gas leaks by observing the bubbling of nitrogen gas at a steady rate where the gas outlet pipe is immersed in water.
CRITICAL STEP When tightly closing the alumina tube with a gas inlet, the tube should be held firmly to avoid changing the position of the alumina combustion boat in the alumina tube.
18 Allow $\geq30$ min to establish the nitrogen atmosphere in the alumina tube.
19 Run the tubular furnace program and check the increase in temperature (Fig. 2a).
! CAUTION Before running the program, double-check that nitrogen gas is bubbling at the outlet to further confirm a stable nitrogen atmosphere.
20 After the temperature has returned to room temperature, close the nitrogen gas line and carefully remove the gas inlet from the alumina tube. Retrieve the alumina combustion boat with the stainless-steel hook.
21 Transfer black NPC from the alumina combustion boat to an $11\mathrm{-mL}$ glass vial. Store NPC in the 11-mL glass vial with its cap closed (Fig. 2d–g).
! CAUTION Seal the gap between the glass vial and cap with Parafilm and store it in the vacuum desiccator for long-term storage.
CRITICAL STEP Direct carbonization generally leads to varying degrees of weight loss in the final carbon product, depending on various factors such as the physical characteristics of precursors, ramping rates, carbonization temperatures and periods. Tracking of carbon yield is therefore highly beneficial to further ensure successful carbonization.
## Procedure 2: synthesis of HZIF-8 and its direct carbonization
## Synthesis of ZIF-8 with diameter ${\sim}120~\mathsf{n m}~\mathrm{~}$ Timing $27\ h$
1 Add $\ensuremath{18.7\mathrm{~g~}}$ of zinc acetate dihydrate and then $200~\mathrm{mL}$ of DIW into a ${500}\mathrm{-mL}$ beaker. Stir the mixture at $400~\mathrm{rpm}$ with a magnetic stirring bar ( $\cdot40\mathrm{-mm}$ length and $8\mathrm{-mm}$ diameter) for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ to fully dissolve zinc acetate dihydrate for preparation of the zinc precursor solution.
2 Add $56.0~\mathrm{g}$ of 2-methylimidazole and then $300~\mathrm{mL}$ of DIW into a separate ${500}\mathrm{-mL}$ beaker. Stir the mixture at $400~\mathrm{rpm}$ with a magnetic stirring bar ( $\mathrm{40-mm}$ length and 8-mm diameter) for $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ to fully dissolve 2-methylimidazole for preparation of the 2-methylimidazole linker solution.
CRITICAL STEP Cover the beaker with Parafilm during stirring to minimize evaporation of water.
Pour the zinc precursor solution into the organic linker solution at a steady rate.
CRITICAL STEP Remove any air bubbles formed at the center of the mixture by using a disposable pipette to minimize potential disruption of nucleation and growth of ZIF-8.
Stir the mixture of the zinc precursor solution and the organic linker solution at $400~\mathrm{rpm}$ for $10~\mathrm{min}$ .
Stop stirring after $10\ \mathrm{\min}$ and remove the magnetic stirring bar ( $_{40-\mathrm{{mm}}}$ length and 8-mm diameter).
Cover the beaker with two layers of Parafilm and age the mixture for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ at room temperature.
After $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , transfer the resulting white solution into $50\mathrm{-mL}$ high-speed PPCO centrifuge tubes.
! CAUTION The weights of the tubes must be well balanced with a maximum weight discrepancy of $0.050~\mathrm{g}$ between the tubes.
