Anticipated results
Procedure 1: synthesis of ZIF-8 and its direct carbonization Detailed characterization of NPC
ZIF-8–derived NPC is obtained in Step 21 of Procedure 1 as illustrated in Fig. $2\mathrm{a}^{52}$ . Figure 2c shows clearly that the six $\left\{100\right\}$ facets become gradually masked by twelve $\{110\}$ facets as single crystalline ZIF-8 grows, thus eventually forming a typical rhombic dodecahedron shape90,91. Uncontrolled random nucleation and subsequent crystal growth, however, produce polycrystalline ZIF-8, which consists of multiple domains and many grain boundaries (Fig. 2b)92. As compared to polycrystalline ZIF-8, single crystalline ZIF-8 is desirable because of its highly ordered crystallographic orientation and uniform thickness. Therefore, single crystalline ZIF-8 is ideal for subsequent nanoarchitecturing to achieve the required improvements in physical and electrochemical properties upon direct carbonization48. NPCs obtained upon direct carbonization of ZIF-8 at the different temperatures of 700, 800, 900 and $1,000~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are denoted as NPC-700, NPC-800, NPC-900 and NPC-1,000, respectively. SEM observations of NPCs indicate that the morphology of single crystalline ZIF-8 particles is strongly retained even after direct carbonization at high temperatures above $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ (Fig. $2\mathrm{d-g)}$ . Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) images of NPCs indicate the homogeneous distribution of carbon and nitrogen contents (Fig. 2d–g). Wide-angle XRD patterns of NPCs carbonized at different temperatures clearly exhibit two broad diffraction peaks at $25^{\circ}$ and $44^{\circ}$ , indicating that they are highly amorphous in nature (Fig. 7a). As carbonization temperature increases from 700 to
$1,000~{^{\circ}C},$ , the relative intensity ratio of Raman-active D and G bands $(I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}})$ increases according to their Raman spectra (Fig. 7b). In Raman spectroscopy, the D band centered at $1,355\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ arises from $\ensuremath{s p}^{3}$ -bonded carbon structures or defects, whereas the G band centered at $1{,}585~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ arises from $\ensuremath{s p}^{2}$ -bonded carbon atoms in a single graphitic sheet. As a result, the increase in $I_{\mathrm{D}}/I_{\mathrm{G}}$ of NPCs upon increasing carbonization temperatures suggests increasing level of amorphousness in the carbon matrix.
Controlling the surface area and porosity of NPC by increasing the carbonization temperature
$\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms of all NPCs obtained at different carbonization temperatures reveal a sharp increase in $\Nu_{2}$ uptake at low relative pressure $_{(<0.05)}$ and gradual increases at relative pressures between 0.3 and 0.9 (Fig. 7c). This pattern of $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption-desorption isotherms of NPCs indicates an abundance of micropores in all the NPCs. Slight hysteresis observed at relative pressures from 0.4 to 1.0 suggests the presence of mesopores in the NPCs. This becomes more obvious according to pore size distributions calculated by non-localized density functional theory (NLDFT), and the percentages of micropores calculated by the $t$ -plot method indicate that pore volumes of the NPCs are dominated by micropores with a significantly lower volume due to mesopores (Fig. 7d). Although almost no morphological variation is observed between the NPCs according to TEM images, their high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) images confirm the presence of mesopores in the carbon matrix (Fig. 7e–l).
Further analyses reveal that carbonization temperature influences both the specific surface area and porosity of the resulting NPCs. Typically, larger specific surface areas and pore volumes are found for NPCs obtained at higher carbonization temperatures from 700 to $900~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . NPC-1,000, however, possesses a small specific surface area and smaller pore volume than NPC-800 despite having been treated at the highest carbonization temperature. This might be due to the significant shrinkage and deformation of the sample occurring at such a high temperature.
## Application: supercapacitor
All NPCs show quasi-rectangular-shaped CV curves at a scan rate of $5~\mathrm{mV/s}$ , although there are distinct differences between the samples in terms of the areas of the curves (Fig. $7\mathrm{m}$ ). Because the area of the CV curve is one of the major parameters in calculating specific capacitance, NPC-900 has the highest specific capacitance of $217.0\ \mathrm{F/g},$ followed by NPC-800 $\mathrm{165.1~F/g})$ , NPC-1,000 $(152.9~\mathrm{F/g)}$ and NPC-700 ( $(15.9\mathrm{F/g})$ (Fig. 7n). Such a low specific capacitance of NPC-700 is possibly due to only partial carbonization at the relatively low carbonization temperature of $700~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
The trend of specific capacitance among the NPCs correlates well with that of their specific surface areas. This is primarily because higher specific surface areas contribute to accommodate greater quantities of electrolyte ions adsorbed onto the carbon, promoting the EDLC charge-storing mechanism to a greater extent. In addition, NPC-900 consistently produces the highest specific capacitance over a range of scan rates from 5 to $500\ \mathrm{mV}/s$ while retaining its capacitance more efficiently than other NPCs obtained at different carbonization temperatures (Fig. 7o).
Procedure 2: synthesis of HZIF-8 and its direct carbonization Detailed characterization of HZIF-8 and its HNPC
HZIF-8 is obtained in Step 20 of Procedure 2, as illustrated in Fig. 3a50. SEM images of ZIF-8 and HZIF-8 are not significantly different in terms of particle size and morphology, indicating that etching by tannic acid does not occur at the surface of ZIF-8 (Fig. $^{3\mathrm{b},\mathrm{c})}$ ). According to their TEM images, however, there are obvious differences in their nanoarchitectures. Typically, ZIF-8 particles possess highly dense interiors, whereas HZIF-8 particles have highly conspicuous hollow cavities at their interiors (Fig. $^{3\mathrm{d},\mathrm{e}}$ ). From SEM and TEM observations, it can therefore be concluded that the interiors of ZIF-8 particles can be selectively etched by using tannic acid. Subsequent direct carbonization of ZIF-8 and HZIF-8 give rise to an NPC and HNPC, respectively. SEM images of both NPC and HNPC reveal their highly homogeneous distributions of particle size (Fig. $\mathrm{8a},\mathrm{c}$ ). In particular, the hollow cavity can easily be observed from the cracked HNPC particles, as indicated by the yellow arrows (Fig. 8c). Moreover, it is observed that ZIF-8 undergoes a significant shrinkage in particle size, although its morphology is relatively well preserved upon direct carbonization to NPC (Fig. 8a,b); however, there is no significant decrease in the particle size of HNPC compared to its precursor. The hollow cavity of ${\sim}95~\mathrm{nm}$ can clearly be observed in its TEM image (Fig. 8d). According to XRD patterns, both NPC and HNPC are amorphous in nature, exhibiting a broad diffraction peak for (002) at ${\sim}25^{\circ}$ (Fig. 8e). C 1s high-resolution XPS (HRXPS) spectra of HNPC and NPC reveal that they are nitrogen-doped carbons as indicated by the presence in the deconvoluted spectrum of C-N bonding (Fig. 8f). Because of the clear difference in nanoarchitectures of NPC and HNPC, their $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/ desorption isotherms and Barrett–Joyner–Halenda (BJH) pore size distributions are dramatically different. According to $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms, both NPC and HNPC accommodate a significant volume of $\Nu_{2}$ molecules at their surfaces at $P/P_{0}<0.05$ , indicating that they are highly microporous in nature. Consequently, NPC and HNPC possess large specific surface areas of 841 and $788~\mathrm{{m}}^{2}/\mathrm{{g}},$ respectively. On the other hand, HNPC shows an additional large uptake of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ molecules at $P/P_{0}$ above 0.90, which can be attributed to its hollow cavity (Fig. 8g). The BJH pore size distribution of NPC exhibits an extremely narrow range of porosity that is primarily limited to micropores, whereas HNPC shows increased pore volume in the mesopore range (pore size from 2 to $20~\mathrm{nm},$ (Fig. 8h). Because HNPC possesses large hollow cavity at its interior, it is expected to have a superior capacitance retention rate over NPC. Capacitance retention rate is an important parameter, especially in supercapacitor performance, because it allows the electrode to operate efficiently at high charge-discharge rates.
## Application: supercapacitor
Procedure 3: synthesis of $Z|\ F{-}8@\mathbf{Z}|\ F{-}67$ and its direct carbonization Detailed characterization of ZIF- $\mathbf{8}@$ ZIF-67 and its $\mathbf{NC}@\mathbf{GC}$
ZIF- $8@$ ZIF-67 is obtained in Step 23 of Procedure 3 as illustrated in Fig. 4a48. SEM images show clearly that the rhombic dodecahedral shape of the ZIF-8 seed is maintained even after the growth of the ZIF-67 shell at a thickness of ${\sim}170~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. $^\mathrm{4b,c}$ ). This is because both ZIF-8 and ZIF-67 have the same crystal structures, allowing direct growth of ZIF-67 at the surface of ZIF-8 (Fig. 4d). The typical core-shell structure is further revealed by EDS imaging of ZIF- $\cdot8@$ ZIF-67 (Figs. 4e and 9a–d). Zinc originating from the ZIF-8 seed is located mainly at the core. Cobalt present in ZIF-67 is located mainly in the shell. The as-synthesized ZIF-8, ZIF-67 and ZIF- $8@$ ZIF-67(0.05 or 0.35) are thermally converted to NC, GC and $\mathrm{NC}@\mathrm{GC}(0.05$ or 0.35), respectively (Fig. 9e–j). The resulting NC particles largely inherit the original rhombic dodecahedron morphology and smooth surfaces of the parent ZIF-8 (Fig. ${5}\mathrm{b,c}$ ). On the other hand, GC particles prepared from ZIF-67 exhibit significant shrinkage with a distorted and bumpy surface (Fig. $\mathrm{5d,e}$ ). Similar observations are made for $\mathtt{N C@G C(0.05)}$ and $\mathrm{NC@GC}(0.35)$ because of the presence of cobalt in the starting ZIF- $8@$ ZIF-67, although the level of surface distortion and roughness varies depending on the thickness of the ZIF-67 shell of the starting ZIF- $3@$ ZIF-67 (Fig. 5f–i). Typically, ZIF-8@ZIF-67(0.35) having a thicker ZIF-67 shell than ZIF$8@$ ZIF-67(0.05) results in $\operatorname{NC@GC}(0.35)$ that shows more severe distortions of its surface compared to $\mathtt{N C@G C(0.05)}$ derived from ZIF- $8@$ ZIF-67(0.05) (Fig. 5f,h). In terms of porosity, no distinct pores are observed in NC because it mainly follows the microporosity of ZIF-8, whereas distinct mesopores can be found in GC and ${\mathrm{NC@GC}}$ particles (Fig. 5b–i). $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms of all samples show varying degrees of $\Nu_{2}$ uptake at a low relative pressure $_{(<0.05)}$ and hysteresis at relative pressures between 0.45 and 1.0, indicating the presence of micropores and mesopores, respectively (Fig. 9k). In detail, specific surface area increases from GC $(496\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ to $\operatorname{NC@GC}(0.35)$ $(813\mathrm{~m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ to $\mathtt{N C@G C(0.05)}$ $(1,276~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ and to NC $(1,499~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})$ , whereas the hysteresis loop becomes more distinct in the reverse order $\begin{array}{r}{\mathrm{(NC~<~NC@GC(0.05)~<~NC@GC(0.35)~<~GC)}.}\end{array}$ As a result, the interconnected hierarchically micro/mesoporous structures appear more distinctively in $\mathtt{N C@G C(0.05)}$ and $\operatorname{NC@GC}(0.35)$ according to pore size distributions (Fig. 9l).
Controlling the thickness of ZIF-67 shell by varying the $\mathbf{Co}^{2+}/\mathbf{Zn}^{2+}$ feed ratio in ZIF-8@ZIF-67 The thickness of the ZIF-67 shell can be easily tuned by increasing the feed ratio of ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}/{\mathrm{Zn}}^{2+}$ precursors during synthesis. Typically, with ZIF-8 seed of $500\mathrm{-nm}$ diameter, the shell thickness increases from 15 to $89\ \mathrm{nm}$ when the feed ratio increases from 0.20 to 1.50 (Fig. 9a,b). When ZIF-8 seed of $4\upmu\mathrm{m}$ is used as the core instead, the shell thickness increases from 33 to $290\mathrm{nm}$ by raising the feed ratio from 0.05 to 0.50 (Fig. 9c,d). This suggests that the amount of cobalt present in the coreshell structure can be effectively controlled by simply increasing its feed ratio, thus allowing subsequent study of the effect of cobalt content in the shell on the final carbon product. Cobalt is known to have a thermal catalytic activity increasing the rate of graphitization of amorphous carbons, so that its presence in the shell of ZIF- $8@$ ZIF-67 can be beneficial during the direct carbonization process.
Controlling the carbon graphitization and nitrogen doping levels by varying the $\mathbf{Co}^{2+}/\mathbf{Zn}^{2+}$ feed ratio in ZIF- $\mathbf{8}@$ ZIF-67
Observations of GC and ${\mathrm{NC@GC}}$ particles by HRTEM reveal that they possess large areas of layered graphitic carbon structures with a layer spacing typical of graphite $(0.334\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) formed on the deposited cobalt nanoparticles by catalytic graphitization of amorphous carbon (Fig. 9f–h)94,95. However, this graphitization of amorphous carbon structures is not observed in NC because it lacks cobalt nanoparticles (Fig. 9e). Wide-angle XRD patterns also correspond to the HRTEM observations because the (002) diffraction peaks of GC, $\mathtt{N C@G C(0.05)}$ and $\mathrm{NC@GC}(0.35)$ are shifted more significantly toward $26^{\circ}$ compared to the broad diffraction peak of NC at $25^{\circ}$ (Fig. 9i). The other intense diffraction peaks at ${\sim}44^{\circ}$ and $51^{\circ}$ are the (111) and (200) diffractions of face-centered cubic cobalt crystals originating from cobalt nanoparticles ( ${\sim}7{-}8~\mathrm{nm}$ in diameter) observed by TEM.
N 1s XPS spectra of NC, GC, $\mathtt{N C@G C(0.05)}$ and $\mathrm{NC}\ @\mathrm{GC}(0.35)$ can be deconvoluted to two peaks at ${\sim}398.9\$ and ${\sim}400.8\ \mathrm{eV}$ corresponding to pyridinic nitrogen and graphitic nitrogen, respectively, thus indicating that these materials consist of nitrogen-doped nanoporous carbon (Fig. 9j). The content of doped nitrogen tends to decrease upon increasing the content of cobalt in the parent MOF because NC has the highest nitrogen content of 16.1 at. $\%$ whereas GC has the lowest nitrogen content of $5.7\ \mathrm{at.\%}$ . Likewise, the nitrogen content of $\mathtt{N C@G C(0.05)}$ $(10.6~\mathrm{at.\%})$ is found to be higher than that of NC@GC(0.35) $(8.5\ \mathrm{at.\%})$ ).
## Application: supercapacitor
Procedure 4: synthesis of $Z|\ F{-}8@\mathsf{m P D A}$ and its direct carbonization Detailed characterization of ZIF- $\mathbf{\delta}\cdot8@\mathbf{mPDA}$ and its HPC
ZIF- $\cdot8@\mathrm{mPDA}$ is obtained in Step 24 of Procedure 4 as illustrated in Fig. ${6\mathrm{a}^{42}}$ . mPDA coating is successfully applied on the surface of ZIF-8 promoted by F127/TMB micellar units. Because the crystalline phase of ZIF-8 is effectively masked by the non-crystalline mPDA shell, diffraction peaks due to ZIF-8 are attenuated in ZIF- $8@\mathrm{mPDA}-2.5$ and ZIF- $8@\mathrm{mPDA}-5.0$ (Extended Data Fig. 3a). For the same reason, XPS spectra also undergo dramatic variations in surface elemental compositions after applying the mPDA coating with significantly reduced intensities of $Z\mathfrak{n}~2p$ and N 1s peaks in ZIF- $\cdot8@\mathrm{mPDA}.-2.5$ and ZIF- $8@\mathrm{mPDA}-5.0$ compared to ZIF-8 (Extended Data Fig. 3b). There is also a significant increase in the intensity of the O 1s peak after coating with mPDA, which originates largely from the quinone/catechol groups of PDA. Interestingly, $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms of ZIF- $\cdot8@\mathrm{mPDA}-2.5$ and ZIF- $8@\mathrm{mPDA}-5.0$ show significantly lower volumes of $\Nu_{2}$ uptake than ZIF8 at low relative pressure $_{(<0.05)}$ (Extended Data Fig. 3c). This is due largely to the blockage of the abundant micropores of ZIF-8 by the mPDA shell leaving almost two-thirds of the micropore volume inaccessible. Consequently, NLDFT pore size distributions of ZIF- $\cdot8@\mathrm{mPDAs}$ also show significantly reduced micropore volumes compared to ZIF-8 (Extended Data Fig. 3d).
Subsequent direct-carbonization processes for ZIF- $8@\mathrm{mPDA}-2.5$ and ZIF- $8@\mathrm{mPDA}-5.0$ result in HPC-2.5 and HPC-5.0, respectively. TEM and SEM images of HPC-5.0 reveal clearly that mPDA is successfully converted into a mesoporous shell whereas the ZIF-8 core disintegrates to form a porous hollow inner shell (Fig. 10a,b). HRTEM images of HPC-5.0 further confirm the presence of mesopores on the surface and inner shell and reveal a scaffold of carbon connecting the mesoporous outer shell and porous hollow inner shell (Fig. 10b). XRD and XPS spectra of NPC, HPC-2.5 and HPC-5.0 indicate that they are amorphous carbon with nitrogen doped in the carbon matrix (Fig. $10c,\mathrm{d}^{\cdot}$ . To identify the state of nitrogen dopants, HRXPS for N 1s is obtained and deconvoluted into four different nitrogen species. All contain a large component of carbon-bonded nitrogen species (graphitic, pyrrolic and pyridinic) (Fig. 10e). As expected from the SEM and TEM images, on the one hand, $\Nu_{2}$ adsorption/desorption isotherms indeed show distinct differences between the NPC and HPCs. Typically, NPC shows a significant volume of $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptake at $P/P_{0}$ below 0.05, indicating it is largely microporous in nature (Fig. 10f). On the other hand, both HPC-2.5 and HPC-5.0 exhibit large hysteresis loops commencing from around $P/P_{0}=0.45$ along with a relatively large $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ uptake in the micropore range (Fig. 10f). Such hysteresis and initial $\Nu_{2}$ uptake generally indicate that the HPCs contain both micropores and mesopores. The presence of both micropores and mesopores is further confirmed by the corresponding NLDFT pore size distributions, which show clear peaks in the micropore and mesopore ranges (Fig. 10g).
## Controlling the thickness of the mPDA coating by varying the polymerization period
At Step 18 of Procedure 4, the mPDA coating begins to form at the surface of ZIF-8 after the initiation of dopamine polymerization upon the addition of Tris. The thickness of mPDA coating can be controlled by varying the polymerization period. TEM images of samples taken after different time periods (6.5, 22, 48 and $^{76\mathrm{~h~}}$ ) demonstrate a clear increase in the thickness of the mPDA coating (Fig. 6h–k). Therefore, the thickness of the mPDA coating can easily be controlled by manipulating the polymerization period to achieve optimized coating conditions for prospective applications.
## Controlling the porosity by varying the volume of TMB
At Step 16 of Procedure 4, the micelle composites are formed after mixing with TMB, which acts as a pore-expanding agent. In the absence of TMB, however, micelle composites cannot form because F127 then acts as a size-limiting agent. Increasing the volume of TMB added increases the numbers of TMB molecules filling the hydrophobic core of the micelles, causing swelling to larger sizes. As a result, we can vary the porosity of the mPDA shell as demonstrated by SEM and TEM images of ZIF$8@\mathrm{mPDA}-2.5$ and ZIF- $\cdot8@\mathrm{mPDA}.5.0$ (Fig. 6b–g). According to the pore size distribution obtained by using ImageJ software, ZIF $-8@\mathrm{mPDA}-2.5$ has a mean pore size of $8~\mathrm{nm}$ , whereas ZI $\mathrm{F}{-}8@\mathrm{mPDA}{-}5.0$ has a mean pore size of $15~\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 6d and g). Therefore, the porosity of the mPDA coating can be controlled by varying the amount of TMB. Controllable porosity is highly desirable, particularly in electrochemical applications.
